----- stdout -----
status-header — every issue/plan doc carries a recognized `**Status:**` header
Status: config-gated

What it checks
  Every doc matching `[status-header] files` carries a `**Status:** <value>` header whose leading keyword is in `live ∪ terminal`. A missing or unrecognized status is reported, never silently treated as non-frozen. A terminal status marks the doc *frozen*: its path citations are exempt from the rules in `suppresses`. Config-driven and inert until `files` is set; not per-path overridable — it is a repo-wide contract.

Config [status-header]
  enabled (default: true) — set false to turn the rule off; scope it per-path with [[overrides]]
  files (default: none (rule inert)) — globs of docs under the contract, e.g. ["issues/**/*.md", "plans/*.md"]; a README under a matched glob is never a tracked item
  header (default: "Status") — the bold label parsed as `**<header>:** <value>`
  live (default: none) — live (non-frozen) status keywords, e.g. ["open", "active"]
  terminal (default: none) — terminal (frozen) status keywords, e.g. ["done", "implemented"]
  suppresses (default: none) — reference rules the frozen exemption skips on a terminal doc; only bare-path, link-case, descriptive-anchor are accepted

Example finding
  status `dnoe` is not a recognized status (expected one of: open, done)

Fix
  No autofix — a finding needs a human decision

